<P>The brute’s friends, quickly abandoned him, fading back into the crowd. Moradorf (apparently his name) glanced back and forth quickly, realizing that something had changed. "Master Khondoran, this halfling was swindlin’ gold fer smithin’ he took me gold and dinna gimme nothin!"</P><br><P>The old shaman stepped quietly into the ring of the crowd and looked about at the faces. He paused at me, and his blue eyes looked deeply into mine, curiously.</P><br><P>"Kheldan!" he called to the younger shaman, "This is a friend of yours, this halfling?"’</P><br><P>"Aye Master, he is my friend from the lowlands of Qeynos—he is a blacksmith."</P><br><P>I looked quizically at the younger mystic. He was younger than me, perhaps five years. His beard and hair were a ruddy blonde, touched with white. I had never seen him before in my life.</P><br><P>Or had I? There was something…familiar about him…</P><br><P>Khondoran continued, "…Is he an honest halfling?"</P><br><P>"Aye, Master, he is." Kheldan replied.</P><br><P>The old shaman turned back to Mordorf, "You ye have anythin’ further, Moradorf?"</P><br><P>Moradorf bowed his head, "Nay, sir."</P><br><P>"Then I think it’s time fer ya to go home."</P><br><P>"Aye. Thank ye sir."</P><br><P>With a wave of his hand the old shaman dismissed the crowd, who quietly, and respectfully disbursed. </P><br><P>I was left to my own to pick up the scattered mess of my things from around the forge.</P><br><P>Well, almost to myself…</P><br><P>"Are you hurt still?" It was Kheldan.</P><br><P>"Hmm…no, not really, just my pride."</P><br><P>"Master Kohndoran’s healing is powerful…but it can’t help that, I fear."</P><br><P>I balanced my pack on the edge of the forge and tried tying it all back together. "So…If I may ask…why did you and your master interfere with that little fight back there. I thought all <B><I>halflings </B></I>were more or less better beaten and burned than not, in this town."</P><br><P>"Well, it wasn’t a fair fight. There were three of them."</P><br><P>I laughed bitterly. "Well, it wouldn’t have been fair if there were just <B><I>one</B></I> of them. You didn’t notice that he was a bit <B><I>bigger </B></I>than me?" I looked the young barbarian up and down. He was not big for a barbarian, but he still towered above me. "Hmph…no I suppose you didn’t. In any event…I’m sorry, I don’t mean to be ungrateful. Thank you for vouching for me. You likely saved my life."</P><br><P>I stopped packing my pack and looked at him again. Even through their crude dress of skins and furs the barbarians were a striking people. The looked rough and noble. But there was something about this one that struck me. He looked so familiar…it was eerie.</P><br><P>Kheldan shifted the tip of his staff in the snow and looked about nervously. When he spoke, his voice was hushed, "Those scars on your arms, where did you come by them?"</P><br><P>"In a drunk wrestling match with a crocodile." I replied.</P><br><P>He scowled, looking puzzled. </P><br><P>I was never any good at humor.</P><br><P>"These scars were given to me in a fight with a monster. The druids of Surefall Glade tell me they cannot be healed."</P><br><P>He nodded knowingly, "What kind of monster?"</P><br><P>I stopped my packing and stared at him. "Why does this interest you. It’s something I’d rather not discuss."</P><br><P>In response he pulled aside his thick bear-hide cloak and lifted up his jherkin. On his chest…from throat to navel was a deep, ragged, greusome scar. "Tis true. The scars the creature leaves can not be healed. This I have had since I was an infant."</P><br><P>It is difficult to make one understand what I felt at this moment. It was sadness, despair….yet mixed with hope….and excitement. Suddenly, I was here with this strange mystic—this barbarian—and we were participating in some kind of rite. Some secret brotherhod pact. For the first time in years I flelt an instant bond form.</P><br><P>Kheldan was a gracious host. I stayed that night in the Shaman’s guild on a straw mat next to the huge stone hearth. The following day, I spent a good deal of the money from the night before on resupplying myself. New fur-lined boots, plenty of arrow points, dried provisions, blade oil and tobbacco were the order of the day. For all the crude amenities in Halas, their tobbacco shop (a place called Dok’s) is very well-stocked and turns a thriving business. The rough-and-tumble warriors of the North may not have the money, day-to-day to care properly for their weapons, but many a goblin and gnoll has met an untimely demise for the sake of a fine cigar. </P><br><P>We talked as friends and were instantly comfortable in the presence of each other. We spoke of our families and our past and our hopes…</P><br><P>&#9;…And, for the first time, we spoke of the creature.</P><br><P>&#9;</P>         <br>
